{
 "translation": "Bedi had completely immersed himself in public service. Despite this, joining in with the voices of his other companions, he certainly used to get the thought that the human heart is so delicate. It can be hurt by even a small thing. It is like the touch-me-not plant, which wilts even if you just extend your hand towards it. But he left no stone unturned in mistreating his Lajwanti. He used to beat her for sitting and standing in inappropriate places, for being careless about eating, and for other such trivial matters. And Lajo was a delicate village girl, like a thin mulberry branch. Due to seeing too much sun, her complexion had become dark. There was a strange restlessness in her nature. Her agitation was like that drop of dew that rolls here and there on a large leaf of the mercury plant. Her thinness was not a sign of her poor health; it was a sign of a healthy body. Seeing this, the heavy-set Sundarlal was initially scared, but when he saw that Lajo could endure every kind of burden, every kind of shock, even beatings, he gradually increased his mistreatment and did not even consider the limits beyond which any human's patience can break. In blurring these boundaries, Lajwanti herself also proved helpful. Since she couldn't stay sad for long, even after the biggest fight, just one smile from Sunderlal would make her unable to stop her laughter, and she would rush to him, put her arms around his neck, and exclaim, \"If you hit me again, I won't speak to you!\" It was clear that she had completely forgotten all the beating. Like other girls in the village, she also knew that men behave this way. In fact, if any woman showed defiance, the girls themselves would put a finger to their nose and say, \"Is that even a man, if a woman doesn't come under his control?\" And this beating had made its way into their songs. Lajjo herself used to sing, \"I won't marry a city boy, he wears boots, and my waist is very thin.\" But at the first opportunity, Lajjo fell in love with a city boy, and his name was Sunderlal, who had come to Lajwanti's village with a wedding procession and who had only whispered this much into the groom's ear: \"Your sister-in-law is so 'salty', friend! Your wife must be 'spicy' too!\" Lajwanti had heard Sundarlal's comment, but she completely forgot how big and ugly Sundarlal's boots were and how thin her own waist was.",
